descartes-discourse-3.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1 has "condemned" and "saw_nothing_prejudicial" and "took_great_care" and "condemned ∧ saw_nothing_prejudicial → ◇fear_error"; descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.3 have "condemned ∧ saw_nothing_prejudicial" and "took_great_care" and "condemned ∧ saw_nothing_prejudicial → fear_error"; they differ when only "persons to whom I greatly defer had condemned a certain doctrine in physics published by another", "previously to their censure I had observed in that doctrine nothing prejudicial to religion or the state", "I always took great care not to believe new opinions without certain demonstrations and not to express aught that might hurt any one" and "possibly, I came to fear lest among my own doctrines some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth" are true, which the first allows [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-3.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S1 is read descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.3 with no modality; descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1 as what is necessary or possible: its "could" or "might" does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-3.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "persons to whom I greatly defer had condemned a certain doctrine in physics published by another", "previously to their censure I had observed in that doctrine nothing prejudicial to religion or the state", "I always took great care not to believe new opinions without certain demonstrations and not to express aught that might hurt any one" and "possibly, I came to fear lest among my own doctrines some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth" are true and "I came to fear lest among my own doctrines some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth" is false. Is S1, "Three years have now elapsed since I finished the treatise containing all these matters; and I was beginning to revise it, with the view to put it into the hands of a printer, when I learned that persons to whom I greatly defer, and whose authority over my actions is hardly less influential than is my own reason over my thoughts, had condemned a certain doctrine in physics, published a short time previously by another individual to which I will not say that I adhered, but only that, previously to their censure I had observed in it nothing which I could imagine to be prejudicial either to religion or to the state, and nothing therefore which would have prevented me from giving expression to it in writing, if reason had persuaded me of its truth; and this led me to fear lest among my own doctrines likewise some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth, notwithstanding the great care I have always taken not to accord belief to new opinions of which I had not the most certain demonstrations, and not to give expression to aught that might tend to the hurt of any one.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1 has "strong_reasons_publish" and "inclination_hostile" and "inclination_hostile → found_excuses" and "◇fear_error ∧ found_excuses → alter_purpose"; descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.3 have "fear_error → alter_purpose" and "strong_reasons_publish" and "inclination_hostile" and "inclination_hostile → found_excuses"; they differ when only "the reasons that had induced me to resolve to publish were very strong", "my inclination has always been hostile to writing books", "my inclination enabled me to discover other considerations sufficient to excuse me from publishing" and "possibly, I came to fear lest among my own doctrines some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth" are true, which the second allows [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-3.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.2 and descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1.3 with no modality; descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means [in 1 of 2 runs]
descartes-discourse-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps descartes-discourse-3.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the reasons that had induced me to resolve to publish were very strong", "my inclination has always been hostile to writing books", "my inclination enabled me to discover other considerations sufficient to excuse me from publishing" and "possibly, I came to fear lest among my own doctrines some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth" are true and "I came to fear lest among my own doctrines some one might be found in which I had departed from the truth" and "I altered my purpose of publishing my treatise" are false. Is S2, "This has been sufficient to make me alter my purpose of publishing them; for although the reasons by which I had been induced to take this resolution were very strong, yet my inclination, which has always been hostile to writing books, enabled me immediately to discover other considerations sufficient to excuse me for not undertaking the task.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
